#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130232} :activity/id #uuid "e92aac25-8e3c-5b47-9748-65ca8bc64600" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705 :topic/id #uuid "6047b0c9-9ae0-5fc8-9179-8ce5172d5c9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1998} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129015 :question/id #uuid "9de92581-00d5-5343-90be-3b5527bfab49" :question/math-academy-id "e-7283"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da85f713-e8f7-5e04-b9e6-b1b33383be9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411866}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Area by Transforming to a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "32924515-5345-5bd8-8b6e-d8d617640311" :step/math-academy-id 26975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130454 :tutorial/content "![](images/8f/8f2d689080add393fad398d45d992e595996a12c3eca60c1c97e9a3e14720ed1.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant (shown above) bounded by the lines\n\n$${\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 3, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 6, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=4, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=6.$$\n\nThe area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$ where $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nLooking at the lines that bound our region, we consider the following change of variables:\n\n$$u = {\\color{blue}{3y-x}}, \\qquad v = {\\color{red}{2x+y}}$$\n\nNote that this change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$\n\nWe substitute $u=3y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines: $$\\begin{aligned} & 3y-x=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\ & 3y-x=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = 2x+y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines: $$\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4 \\\\ & 2x+y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 6, \\quad 4 \\leq v \\leq 6\\big\\}$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d55142f7713f3a9bfb93d1b1a383683af397a6edcd7c56a4338f25ab16b83a2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is $$u = 3y-x, \\qquad v = 2x+y.$$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =-7.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is $$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac17.$$ Note that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables: $$\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{7}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (6-3)\\cdot (6-4) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0b502c14-a087-54fa-8a4d-3bda73b991ca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7037 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "5cd583ad-9fe0-5183-8354-e66c520bd7ad" :step/math-academy-id 42405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128836 :question/id #uuid "418bea54-7fb3-5013-b91c-355a45af08e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371913}] :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"} :step/id #uuid "51476dcf-0d6d-5530-9345-e9f13c6e0c72" :step/math-academy-id 42406 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128837 :question/id #uuid "acd76e31-5c7e-5577-a402-f003b3c2e1bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-11154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444d700-8c19-5712-8cf5-99555938b0c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371974}] :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "5e3d0649-15dd-5adf-828b-27ae3bc49e74" :step/math-academy-id 42417 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130464 :tutorial/content "Given a real interval $D = (a,b)\\subset \\mathbb R,$ the length of this interval can be represented by the integral\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int_a^b \\,\\textrm d x$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x.$$\n\nSuppose we define an invertible function $u = u(x).$ By the change of variables formula, the length of our interval can also be expressed as\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Whenever we perform a change of variables, the domain of integration changes, too. This new integration domain is denoted $\\Delta.$ More technically, $\\Delta$ is the *image* of the $D$ under the action of $u(x).$\n- If we think of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}$ as a fraction, the $\\textrm d u$'s in our length formula appear to \"cancel out,\" leaving only the original $\\textrm d x.$ This makes the change of variables formula easy to remember.\n\nSo, to summarize,\n\n$$\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.$$\n\nSince the length of our interval is simply $b-a,$ it's doubtful that we'll ever use this formula in practice! However, it does indicate how we might use a change of variables to find the *area* of a complex region in two-dimensional space. Let's explore this idea in more detail." :tutorial/id #uuid "11fb8887-a8b0-514d-b23a-0cc6931ba17b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7682 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f59c81ad-5b44-5523-9aeb-a14b98235004" :step/math-academy-id 44616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130465 :tutorial/content "![](images/6e/6ee5a19bf58db28574dc82aa3e6adcfd59015777bc503e66b9b9fd427278dcbf.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. The area of this region is given by\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y$$\n\nwhere we'll use the convention $\\textrm d A = \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y$ when referring to integration with respect to the Cartesian coordinates $x$ and $y.$\n\nThis region looks pretty complicated. In such cases, it's often easier to find an invertible transformation $\\mathbf T$ (i.e., change of variables) whose inverse maps the region $D$ to simpler, preferably rectangular, region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\nSo, rather than computing the required area by integrating with respect to $x$ and $y,$ we can instead perform the transformation and integrate with respect to $u$ and $v.$\n\nTo carry out this process, we apply the **change of variables formula**\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNotice that this formula is analogous to the one we saw earlier for computing the length of an interval. In particular:\n\n- If we consider the Jacobian as a kind of fraction, the $\\partial(u,v)$ and $\\textrm d u\\textrm d v$ parts appear to \"cancel out,\" similar to the previous formula.\n- Note that $\\mathbf T$ could be orientation-reversing. We take the absolute value of the Jacobian to ensure that $D$ and $\\Delta$ have the same orientation.\n\nFinally, note that the change of variables formula assumes that $\\mathbf T$ is a $C^1$ transformation and has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$\n\nOften, the most challenging part is finding a suitable change of variables. The main idea is to get a nice rectangular shape with sides parallel to the axes in the $uv$-plane. In other words, we require a change of variables that reduces the equations of our curves to something like the following: $$u = \\textrm{const}_1, \\quad u = \\textrm{const}_2, \\quad v = \\textrm{const}_3, \\quad v = \\textrm{const}_4$$\n\nLet's look at a concrete example of computing an area using the change of variables formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5fba407-4d6f-55e7-a93a-4f1e6fe6369f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7683 :tutorial/title "The Change of Variables Formula"} :step/id #uuid "4aa36004-91ee-5911-b639-83ff0faef28a" :step/math-academy-id 44617 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130199}}] :activity/title "Computing Areas Using a Change of Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129343}]}
